  "content": {
    "id": "p42-q5",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Solve the following simultaneous equations, making use of the quadratic formula.\nLeave answers in exact surd form. Check your answer using DESMOS.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p42-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the point of intersection of\n$$y=2x^2+3x+6\\text{ and }y=x+4.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No point of intersection.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2x^2+3x+6&=x+4\\\\2x^2+2x+2&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{2^2-4(2)(2)}}{2(2)}\\\\x&=\\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{-12}}{4}\\end{align*}$$\nThe square root is not real, so there is no point of intersection. In DESMOS, the graphs have no common point.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the point of intersection of\n$$y=x^2+2x-5\\text{ and }y=x.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\frac{-1-\\sqrt{21}}{2},\\frac{-1-\\sqrt{21}}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{21}}{2},\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{21}}{2}\\right)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2+2x-5&=x\\\\x^2+x-5&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{1^2-4(1)(-5)}}{2(1)}\\\\x&=\\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{21}}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $y=x$, the points are $\\left(\\frac{-1-\\sqrt{21}}{2},\\frac{-1-\\sqrt{21}}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{21}}{2},\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{21}}{2}\\right)$.\nFor the DESMOS check, these correspond to approximately $(-2.79,-2.79)$ and $(1.79,1.79)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 75
      }
    ]
  },
